**Complex implementation and verification** cover changes across coupled components with a known design and checkable invariants. Use this intermediate tier for Implement and Verify Impl. Record the workflow shape as Standard and name the Complex route in the handoff WHY when applicable. Plan and Verify Plan still use the Standard rows. Deep still covers ambiguous invariants, hard concurrency or changes whose correctness is difficult to verify.

**Shape test.** Micro requires an explicit outcome, tiny named surface, no discovery and a direct checker. Bounded requires a small familiar surface, close specification, reversibility and strong acceptance criteria. Standard begins with meaningful exploration, cross-file coupling, debugging, non-trivial design or a weak success signal. Deep is ambiguous long-horizon work, security architecture, difficult concurrency or consequential migration design.

Do not restart a stage merely because its shape rises. Continue safe inspection, then reroute the next owner/run when the current route lacks enough capability. A cheaper attempt that produces usable evidence is not wasted; include that evidence in the reroute. After one failure, do not repeat the materially identical attempt.

Exposure controls action gates, not model intelligence. A Consequential but tiny action may be Micro yet still needs its boundary check; a difficult Local refactor may be Deep without requiring permission. Advisor picks: see **Advisor route**. Fix follows Implement; Plan Next follows Plan.

## Worker routes

Workers follow the `delegate` skill. Choose by task fit, then allowance.{{if .Both}} The Claude route is the default among comparable routes; either provider's owner may launch either provider.{{end}} Batch related tasks; use direct tools for deterministic search and substitutions when they suffice.

| Bounded task |{{if .Claude}} Claude Code |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Codex |{{end}}
|---|{{if .Claude}}---|{{end}}{{if .Codex}}---|{{end}}
| Exact extraction, mechanical log summary |{{if .Claude}} Haiku 5.5 low |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol low; Luna low only for very large volume |{{end}}
| Repetitive specified edits with a direct checker |{{if .Claude}} Haiku 5.5 high |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol low |{{end}}
| Bounded semantic execution, test-log triage |{{if .Claude}} Sonnet 5.5 high; Haiku 5.5 xhigh only for checkable tasks it can handle |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol medium |{{end}}
| Source collection, web research |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 medium, read-and-web-only child where the transport allows |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol high |{{end}}
| Quick sanity check against supplied criteria |{{if .Claude}} Haiku 5.5 high for fixed checks; Opus 5.5 low for fresh judgment |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol low for fixed checks and fresh judgment |{{end}}
| Multi-file bounded work with a checker |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 medium |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol medium; high when diagnosis or terminal work warrants it |{{end}}
| Research synthesis (quality first) |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 xhigh; high for a narrow, well-sourced question |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} {{if .Both}}Astra high only under its gate{{else}}Sol max; Astra high only under its gate{{end}} |{{end}}
| Hard or long-horizon implementation in its own worktree |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 xhigh |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol xhigh; max when its measured advantage fits the task; Astra high only under its gate |{{end}}

Fan-out size never lowers the capability a task needs. These are starting points; the owner resolves conflicting evidence and keeps acceptance.

**Orchestrating owner.** An owner whose main work is directing workers, where delegation has a concrete benefit under `delegate`, is an ordinary stage owner, not a stage: it decomposes, writes packets with finite acceptance criteria, launches bounded waves, verifies each result, integrates serially and runs the combined checks; it may do scoped work directly, and its own checking does not replace a fresh Verify Impl.{{if .Claude}} The default {{if .Both}}Claude {{end}}orchestrator is Opus 5.5, medium for specified execution and high when decomposition, acceptance criteria or integration need judgment.{{end}} It may consult an advisor (see **Advisor route**) at the large steps: the decomposition before the first fan-out, ordering work, a merge decision on conflicting results, or when stuck. It acts on the advice and keeps decision authority; it does not hand the work over. Pick each worker from the table above by its own task, never by the owner's model, with an explicit model and effort (a native subagent without a model inherits the owner's). Every owner turn re-reads its whole context, so wait on a wave once rather than taking a turn per child, and keep bulk reads and fetched pages in workers.

## Plan allowance

These routes were generated from the plan you declared; agentfile never inspects accounts or queries live usage. A declared plan is a routing hint, not proof that a model is enabled in the account.

{{range .Allowance}}- **{{.Client}} — {{.Plan}}.** {{.Guidance}} {{.Credits}}
{{end}}{{if .OrderNote}}
{{.OrderNote}}
{{end}}
Plan allowance is not API price, and no vendor publishes a per-effort allowance multiplier.{{if .Codex}} [OpenAI](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing) estimates local messages per 5 hours on Plus and Standard Business: GPT-6.2 Sol 17–180, GPT-6.2 Astra 6–50 and Luna 350–3,000 (estimates vary by task); ChatGPT Pro has no 5-hour limit.{{end}}{{if .Claude}} Claude session and weekly limits are shared across all models and across Claude and Claude Code, and subagents count toward them; Anthropic says only that higher effort uses more tokens and that Haiku and Sonnet cost fewer tokens than Opus. [Claude Code's error docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/errors.md) mention separate Opus-family and Sonnet-family limits without saying which plans have them; after such a limit, a model outside that family may still run.{{end}}

First select every route capable of the task; the declared plan may only order comparable suitable routes and decide how sparingly to spend a limited allowance. It never makes an incapable route eligible, never lowers the capability rung the task requires, and never makes an expensive model the default without a task-specific reason.

When the client reports a usage limit, or a recommended model is unavailable in the client or account, take the next capable route in the table{{if .Both}} (including the other provider's route){{end}}{{if .Claude}}, or a listed allowance fallback where the work fits{{end}}. Classify the limit by its scope: an account-wide limit excludes that account on every transport; a model-family limit excludes only that family. If no route remains, stop and tell the user which model or allowance is missing. Never invent a route from a model name or plan label.

## Advisor route

Advisors are {{.AdvisorList}}. {{.NonAdvisors}}

| Question |{{if .Claude}} Claude Code |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Codex |{{end}}
|---|{{if .Claude}}---|{{end}}{{if .Codex}}---|{{end}}
| Quick fresh look at something simple; the answer is verifiable on sight |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 low |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol low |{{end}}
| Bounded: narrow, reversible, one decision, evidence supplied |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 medium |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol medium |{{end}}
| Standard: tradeoffs, design choice, diagnosis from incomplete evidence |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 high |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol high |{{end}}
| Hard, large or high-stakes: security architecture, concurrency, irreversible migration, a packet bundling many decisions, a review across a whole batch of changes |{{if .Claude}} Opus 5.5 xhigh |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} Sol xhigh, only if the scoped question fits its evidence; Astra high only under its gate |{{end}}
| The row above fell short |{{if .Claude}} {{if .Both}}Reassess with another provider; {{end}}Opus 5.5 max |{{end}}{{if .Codex}} {{if .Both}}Narrow the question or reassess with another provider{{else}}Sol max for knowledge-heavy synthesis; otherwise narrow the question{{end}}; Astra high only under its gate |{{end}}
{{if .Both}}
- Prefer the provider other than the executing owner's; for advisors, provider diversity outranks the Claude default. A Codex owner asks Opus; a Claude Code owner asks Sol, or Astra high under its gate on the hardest row. Diversity does not establish capability equivalence (on the index, Astra high 60, Opus xhigh 57, Sol xhigh 56): if no sufficiently capable other-provider route is established for the question, say so rather than claiming a weaker one meets the need. Start with one advisor; for a hard or high-stakes decision, one per provider is often worth it.
- Same-provider advice is allowed and labeled; it never meets a cross-provider requirement. Never the executing process.
{{- else}}
- Only {{.OnlyProvider}} models are selected, so every advisor is same-provider: label it so. An explicit cross-provider requirement cannot be met with the selected agents: stop and ask the user. Never the executing process.
{{- end}}
- Use a native read-only child for same-provider advice, or the CLI (`delegate`).{{if .Codex}}
- **Astra gate:** Astra needs a demonstrated hard need, capable regular routes that failed or are unsuitable, and explicit per-scope user approval, and its routes stop at high. That ceiling limits the routes you choose, not the user: when the user names Astra at xhigh or max, run it at the named effort. Ask for approval with the specific frontier need and continue safe work while it is pending; no reply or a refusal means Astra is not granted and leaves {{if .Both}}Opus and Sol{{else}}Sol{{end}} advice available.{{end}}
- Effort follows how hard and how costly the decision is, not how important the task feels. When torn between two rows, take the lower and escalate one row only when the answer is shallow or misses the point you asked about; that is falling short, not a re-ask. A fan-out wave gives each advisor the row for its own question, not the batch's.{{if .Both}} Medium → high adds 4 index points for Opus and 2 for Sol; Sol high → xhigh adds 1 and improves FrontierCode 2.0 while Terminal-Bench 4.0 falls. Low trails medium by about 12 points for both, so keep it for quick looks.{{else if .Claude}} Opus medium → high is +4 index at about twice the time to first token; low is 13 points below medium, so keep it for quick looks.{{else}} Sol medium → high adds 2 index points (3.0 s vs 9.6 s to first token), and high → xhigh adds 1 and improves FrontierCode 2.0 while Terminal-Bench 4.0 falls; low is 12 points below medium, so keep it for quick looks.{{end}} Compact packets cut input, not reasoning output: choose effort for the question. Route or transport changes never change model quality or provider diversity, and are never a reason to re-ask.
